     "body": "Found while checking magpie-community/plugins#90 (agent-kilo) against magpie main 65ee8c65c.\n\n## What happens\n\nAn agent plugin wired to an agent, then turned off (`magpie plugin off <name>`) or removed, leaves the agent's config pointing at magpie. magpie then no longer knows the agent, so it can't put the config back:\n\n```\n$ magpie kilo magpie/fakeup/fake-chat     # kilo.jsonc gets provider.magpie + model\n$ magpie plugin off .../packages/kilo\n$ magpie kilo default\nmagpie: unknown agent \"kilo\" (try: agy, aionui, …)\n```\n\n`kilo.jsonc` keeps `\"model\": \"magpie/fakeup/fake-chat\"` and the whole `provider.magpie` block. The stash in plugged.go that would restore it is only reached through the agent, which is gone with the plugin.\n\nSwitching the agent off first (`magpie kilo default`) restores the file byte for byte, so the plugin itself is fine. The gap is in `SetPluginOff` / `RemovePlugin` (internal/provider/migrate.go), which call `plugin.SetOff` / `plugin.Remove` without disconnecting an agent the plugin defines. Every agent plugin has it: aider, pig, type4me, cowork, kimi-desktop, kilo.\n\n## Expected\n\nTurning an agent plugin off or removing it first puts back what the user had under every key magpie wrote (as `magpie <agent> default` does), or refuses with a message saying to switch the agent off first. Either way the user is never left with a config only magpie could undo.\n\nSandbox: HOME under magpie-wt, gateway on 3481, fake upstream; Kilo CLI 7.8.18.\n",
